  'Palatine Germans settled this stretch of the Mohawk in the early 1700s; a marker on East Main Street names Johann Jacob Zimmerman as founder in 1725, with his mill of 1757, a Revolutionary fort and a turnpike tavern of 1800 on the same ground. The village, on the north bank of the river and the Erie Canal, was incorporated in 1857, and in 2019 almost all of it was listed on the National Register as the St. Johnsville Historic District, which traces its growth from an 18th-century mill site into a 19th-century industrial town (Wikipedia).',
  'Main Street (NY 5) and the streets north of it, with four buildings listed on their own: the Community House (the Bates-Englehardt mansion), the Methodist church, the library in its park, and the 1936 post office. Zimmerman Creek runs through the village. The Zimmerman marker is at 7 East Main Street, east of Bridge Street. Which mills and factory buildings survive was not checked.',
  'A whole canal-and-mill village on the Register, nearly every street of it.',
  'Public streets. Village lots on Center Street and Park Place, off Main Street (OpenStreetMap). The Canalway Trail and the marina are south of the railroad.',
